steps.body:
TypeScript widens what it infers from an array literal: `["/", "/about"]` passed to `<T extends readonly string[]>` gives `T` the type `string[]`. Write `const` before the type parameter, and the argument is inferred as if it had `as const`, a readonly tuple of literals:

```typescript
function routes<const T extends readonly string[]>(paths: T): T {
  return paths;
}

const site = routes(["/", "/about"]);
//    ^? const site: readonly ["/", "/about"]
console.log(site.length); // 2
```

When a type parameter appears in several parameters, every argument can help infer it. `NoInfer<S>` marks a parameter that must not: it is only checked against the `S` that the other arguments decided.

Overloads give one function several call signatures, written above its single implementation. They suit calls that really differ in shape. When the result is just a lookup by key, one generic signature with `K extends keyof T` does the same job and grows with the type.

examples.code:
function routes<const T extends readonly string[]>(paths: T): T {
  return paths;
}

const site = routes(["/", "/about", "/shop"]);
//    ^? const site: readonly ["/", "/about", "/shop"]
type Route = (typeof site)[number];
//   ^? type Route = "/" | "/about" | "/shop"

function link(to: Route, text: string): string {
  return `<a href="${to}">${text}</a>`;
}

console.log(site.join(" "));
console.log(link("/shop", "Shop"));

examples.explanation:
With `const`, the argument keeps its literal types, so `site` is a readonly tuple of the three paths, and `(typeof site)[number]` turns it into their union. `link` accepts only those paths, so `link("/shpo", "Shop")` is a type error. Without `const`, the same call infers plain `string[]`, and `Route` would be any string:

```typescript
function routes<T extends readonly string[]>(paths: T): T {
  return paths;
}

const site = routes(["/", "/about", "/shop"]);
//    ^? const site: string[]
console.log(site.length); // 3
```

`const` only changes how a literal written in the call is inferred. An array you stored in a variable first is already a `string[]`, so pass the literal straight in.

examples.code:
function createMachine<S extends string>(states: readonly S[], initial: NoInfer<S>) {
  return { states, current: initial };
}

const player = createMachine(["idle", "playing", "stopped"], "paused");
console.log(player.current);

examples.explanation:
A type parameter constrained to `string` keeps the literal strings it's given, and `NoInfer` lets only `states` decide them. So `S` is `"idle" | "playing" | "stopped"`, and `"paused"` is rejected. Without `NoInfer`, `"paused"` would count as one more state, `S` would grow to include it, and the mistake would compile and run:

```typescript
function createMachine<S extends string>(states: readonly S[], initial: S) {
  return { states, current: initial };
}

const player = createMachine(["idle", "playing", "stopped"], "paused");
//    ^? const player: { states: readonly ("idle" | "paused" | "playing" | "stopped")[]; current: "idle" | "paused" | "playing" | "stopped"; }
console.log(player.current); // paused
```

